Here's a simple decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the NODE_ENV variable and calls the next() function if it's equal to 'true', otherwise it returns false:

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvGuard implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = req.nodeEnv;

    return nodeEnv === 'true';
  }
}
```

You can use this decorator like this:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get, UseGuards } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvGuard } from './env.guard';

@Controller('your-controller-path')
export class YourController {
  @Get()
  @UseGuards(EnvGuard)
  yourAction() {
    // Your action code here
  }
}
```

This will ensure that the `yourAction` method is only executed when the NODE_ENV variable is set to 'true'. Otherwise, it will return false and the request will be handled by the default error handler.

