To make the planets less crowded, you can add a minimum distance between them during the generation process. Here's an updated version of the `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

In this updated function, we added a `minDistance` parameter that specifies the minimum distance between two planets. When generating the planets, we check if the new planet is too close to any existing planet using this distance. If it is, we skip that planet and generate a new one.

You can call this function with a suitable value for `minDistance` to control the spacing between the planets. For example, you could set `minDistance` to 10 or 20 units, depending on the size of your planets and the overall scale of your scene.

