To update your .NET 7 application without replacing the existing EXE, you can follow these steps:

1. Create a new folder in the target directory where you want to install the update. Name it something like "Update".
2. Extract the contents of the ZIP file into the "Update" folder.
3. Modify the existing EXE file to check for updates and apply them if available. You can use the `System.IO` and `System.Diagnostics` namespaces to read the version information from the EXE file and compare it with the version number of the updated files.
4. If an update is available, you can copy the updated files over the existing ones, while preserving the original files' permissions and attributes.
5. Restart the application to apply the updates.

Here's some sample code to get you started:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Diagnostics;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Check for updates
        string exePath = Path.GetFullPath(Process.GetCurrentProcess().MainModule.FileName);
        string updateFolder = Path.Combine(exePath, "Update");

        if (Directory.Exists(updateFolder))
        {
            // Update found, apply it
            ApplyUpdate(exePath, updateFolder);
        }
        else
        {
            // No update found, continue with the existing application
            Console.WriteLine("No update found.");
        }
    }

    static void ApplyUpdate(string exePath, string updateFolder)
    {
        // Copy updated files over the existing ones
        foreach (string file in Directory.GetFiles(updateFolder))
        {
            string destinationPath = Path.Combine(exePath, file);
            File.Copy(file, destinationPath, true);
        }

        // Restart the application
        Process.Start(exePath);
        Environment.Exit(0);
    }
}
```
Replace the `ApplyUpdate` method with your own logic to handle the actual update process, such as copying specific files or modifying the application's configuration.

