Here's a PowerShell script that recursively checks folders for ACLs referring to SIDs in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain:

```powershell
# Define the function to check the ACLs of a folder and its subfolders
function CheckFolderACLs($folderPath) {
    $acl = Get-Acl -Path $folderPath
    foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
        if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-') {
            # Check if the SID is in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain
            $domain = (Get-ADDomain).Name
            $sid = New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($access.IdentityReference)
            $sidHistory = $sid.Translate([System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier]::Format(2))
            if ((Get-ADUser -Filter {SIDHistory -eq $sidHistory} -Properties SIDHistory).Count -gt 0) {
                Write-Host "The ACL of $folderPath references a SID ($sid) that occurs only in the SIDHistory of a user or group in the domain."
            }
        }
    }
    foreach ($subFolder in (Get-ChildItem -Path $folderPath -Directory)) {
        CheckFolderACLs($subFolder.FullName)
    }
}

# Define the root folder to start checking
$rootFolder = "C:\YourRootFolder"

# Check the ACLs of the root folder and its subfolders
CheckFolderACLs($rootFolder)
```

Replace `C:\YourRootFolder` with the root folder you want to start checking. This script will recursively check all folders within the specified root folder and its subfolders for ACLs referencing SIDs in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain.
